# Permissions and Access Control with API Keys

Bknd provides a flexible authorization system that can be adapted to various authentication methods. This guide focuses on using API keys for access control and guarding your data endpoints.

## What You'll Learn

- Configure the Guard with API key authentication
- Define roles and permissions for API key holders
- Use policies for row-level security based on key metadata
- Implement public endpoints with key-based restrictions
- Filter data based on API key context

## Core Concepts

Bknd's authorization hierarchy remains the same:

```
Guard (evaluates)
  └─> Roles (group permissions)
      └─> Permissions (define what's allowed)
          └─> Policies (conditional logic)
```

But the authentication source changes: instead of user JWT tokens, you use API keys stored in a dedicated table.

## Enabling Authorization with API Keys

You can replace default JWT auth with an API key authentication handler:

```typescript
import { em, entity, text, boolean, createAuthHandler } from "bknd";

const schema = em({
  api_keys: entity("api_keys", {
    key: text().required(),
    role: text(), // e.g., "guest", "editor"
    owner: text(), // optional, for ownership
  }),
  posts: entity("posts", {
    title: text().required(),
    content: text(),
    published: boolean(),
    owner_id: text(), // matching api_keys.owner
  }),
});

const apiKeyAuth = createAuthHandler({
  name: "api-key",
  authenticate: async (ctx) => {
    const key = ctx.request.headers.get("X-API-Key");
    if (!key) return null;
    const apiKey = await ctx.em.findOne("api_keys", { key });
    if (!apiKey) return null;
    // Create a user-like context from the API key
    return {
      id: apiKey.id,
      role: apiKey.role,
      owner: apiKey.owner,
    };
  },
});

export default {
  config: {
    data: schema.toJSON(),
    auth: {
      enabled: true,
      handler: apiKeyAuth, // custom handler
      roles: [
        {
          name: "guest",
          is_default: true,
          implicit_allow: false,
          permissions: [
            {
              permission: "data.entity.read",
              effect: "allow",
              policies: [
                {
                  condition: { entity: "posts" },
                  effect: "filter",
                  filter: { published: true },
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          name: "editor",
          is_default: false,
          implicit_allow: false,
          permissions: [
            {
              permission: "data.entity.create",
              effect: "allow",
              policies: [
                {
                  condition: { entity: "posts" },
                  effect: "allow",
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              permission: "data.entity.update",
              effect: "allow",
              policies: [
                {
                  condition: { entity: "posts" },
                  effect: "filter",
                  filter: { owner_id: "@auth.owner" },
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              permission: "data.entity.delete",
              effect: "allow",
              policies: [
                {
                  condition: { entity: "posts" },
                  effect: "filter",
                  filter: { owner_id: "@auth.owner" },
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
    },
  },
};
```

## Defining Roles for API Keys

Roles are assigned when you create an API key in the `api_keys` table:

```typescript
{
  name: "editor",
  is_default: false,
  implicit_allow: false,
  permissions: [...]
}
```

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `name` | string | Matches the `role` field in the api_keys row |
| `is_default` | boolean | Used for keys without an explicit role |
| `implicit_allow` | boolean | Dangerous; avoid in production |
| `permissions` | array | Permissions for this role |

## Permission Effects

Same as standard Bknd, but with `@auth.owner` instead of `@auth.user.id`.

### Allow Effect

```typescript
{
  permission: "data.entity.create",
  effect: "allow",
  policies: [
    {
      condition: { entity: "posts" },
      effect: "allow",
    },
  ],
}
```

### Filter Effect

```typescript
{
  permission: "data.entity.update",
  effect: "allow",
  policies: [
    {
      condition: { entity: "posts" },
      effect: "filter",
      filter: { owner_id: "@auth.owner" },
    },
  ],
}
```

## Common Patterns

### Public Read, Authenticated Write (API Key Based)

The configuration above already implements this:
- Guest role (default for requests without a valid API key) sees only `published: true` posts.
- Editor role (valid API key with `role: "editor"`) can create posts and update/delete only their own posts (matched via `owner_id`).

### API Key Ownership Pattern

Each API key has an `owner` field; posts store the same value in `owner_id`. This mimics user ownership without a full user system.

### Multi-Tenant Isolation Using API Keys

```typescript
{
  name: "tenant_admin",
  permissions: [
    {
      permission: "data.entity.read",
      effect: "allow",
      policies: [
        {
          condition: { entity: "*" },
          effect: "filter",
          filter: { tenant_id: "@auth.owner" },
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
}
```

## Policy Variables

| Variable | Source | Example |
|----------|--------|---------|
| `@auth.id` | API key row ID | `@auth.id` |
| `@auth.role` | Role from key | `@auth.role` |
| `@auth.owner` | `owner` field of the API key | `@auth.owner` |
| `@ctx.*` | Custom context | `@ctx.environment` |

## Testing Permissions with API Keys

Create test API keys and verify access:

```typescript
import { createApp } from "bknd";

