- **[Data Schema](data-schema)** - Define your data model
- **[Query](query)** - Learn the query system

# Permissions and Access Control

Bknd provides a flexible authorization system based on Guard, roles, permissions, and policies. This system controls access to your application's resources.

## What You'll Learn

- Configure the Guard and roles  
- Define permissions with allow and deny effects  
- Use policies for conditional logic  
- Implement guest vs. authenticated user access  
- Secure data operations with role-based permissions

## Core Concepts

Bknd's authorization follows this hierarchy:

```
Guard (evaluates)
  └─> Roles (group permissions)
      └─> Permissions (define what's allowed)
          └─> Policies (conditional logic)
```

- **Guard**: Evaluates permissions against user context  
- **Roles**: Group permissions and set default behavior  
- **Permissions**: Grant access with allow or deny effects  
- **Policies**: Add conditional conditions to permissions

## Enabling Authorization

The Guard is automatically enabled when you enable auth:

```typescript
import { em, entity, text, boolean } from "bknd";

const schema = em({
  posts: entity("posts", {
    title: text().required(),
    content: text(),
    published: boolean(),
  }),
});

export default {
  config: {
    data: schema.toJSON(),
    auth: {
      enabled: true,
      jwt: {
        issuer: "my-app",
      },
      roles: [
        {
          name: "guest",
          is_default: true,
          implicit_allow: false,
          permissions: [
            {
              permission: "data.entity.read",
              effect: "allow",
              policies: [
                {
                  condition: { entity: "posts" },
                  effect: "allow",
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
        },
        {
          name: "user",
          is_default: false,
          implicit_allow: false,
          permissions: [
            {
              permission: "data.entity.create",
              effect: "allow",
              policies: [
                {
                  condition: { entity: "posts" },
                  effect: "allow",
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              permission: "data.entity.update",
              effect: "allow",
              policies: [
                {
                  condition: { entity: "posts" },
                  effect: "allow",
                },
              ],
            },
            {
              permission: "data.entity.delete",
              effect: "allow",
              policies: [
                {
                  condition: { entity: "posts" },
                  effect: "allow",
                },
              ],
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
    },
  },
};
```

In this configuration:
- Anyone (guests) can read all posts, regardless of published status.
- Authenticated users can create, update, and delete any post.

This provides a simple role-based system but does not implement row-level filtering for published posts or ownership-based restrictions.

## Defining Roles

Roles group permissions and set default behavior:

```typescript
{
  name: "admin",
  is_default: false,
  implicit_allow: true,
  permissions: [],
}
```

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `name` | string | Unique role identifier |
| `is_default` | boolean | Assigned to users without explicit role |
| `implicit_allow` | boolean | Allow all permissions (use with caution) |
| `permissions` | array | List of permissions for this role |

## Permission Effects

Permissions define what's allowed using two effects:

### Allow Effect

Grants access when the policy condition is met:

```typescript
{
  permission: "data.entity.read",
  effect: "allow",
  policies: [
    {
      condition: { entity: "posts" },
      effect: "allow",
    },
  ],
}
```

### Deny Effect

Revokes access (takes precedence over allow):

```typescript
{
  permission: "data.entity.delete",
  effect: "deny",
  policies: [
    {
      condition: { entity: "posts" },
      effect: "deny",
    },
  ],
}
```

## Common Patterns

### Public Read, Authenticated Write (Simplified)

```typescript
{
  auth: {
    enabled: true,
    roles: [
      {
        name: "guest",
        is_default: true,
        implicit_allow: false,
        permissions: [
          {
            permission: "data.entity.read",
            effect: "allow",
            policies: [
              {
                condition: { entity: "posts" },
                effect: "allow",
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
      {
        name: "user",
        is_default: false,
        implicit_allow: false,
        permissions: [
          {
            permission: "data.entity.create",
            effect: "allow",
            policies: [
              {
                condition: { entity: "posts" },
                effect: "allow",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            permission: "data.entity.update",
            effect: "allow",
            policies: [
              {
                condition: { entity: "posts" },
                effect: "allow",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            permission: "data.entity.delete",
            effect: "allow",
            policies: [
              {
                condition: { entity: "posts" },
                effect: "allow",
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
}
```

This gives guests full read access to all posts and authenticated users full write access.

### User Role with Full CRUD

```typescript
{
  name: "user",
  permissions: [
    {
      permission: "data.entity.read",
      effect: "allow",
      policies: [
        {
          condition: { entity: "posts" },
          effect: "allow",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      permission: "data.entity.create",
      effect: "allow",
      policies: [
        {
          condition: { entity: "posts" },
          effect: "allow",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      permission: "data.entity.update",
      effect: "allow",
      policies: [
        {
          condition: { entity: "posts" },
          effect: "allow",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      permission: "data.entity.delete",
      effect: "allow",
      policies: [
        {
          condition: { entity: "posts" },
          effect: "allow",
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
}
```

## Policy Variables

Policies support dynamic variable substitution using `@variable` syntax:

| Variable | Source | Example |
|----------|--------|---------|
| `@auth.user.id` | Authenticated user's ID | `@auth.user.id` |
| `@auth.user.role` | User's role name | `@auth.user.role` |
| `@auth.user.*` | Any user property | `@auth.user.email`, `@auth.user.tenant_id` |
| `@ctx.*` | Guard config context | Custom context variables |

## Data Permissions

Bknd provides built-in permissions for data operations:

| Permission | Description | Filterable |
|------------|-------------|------------|
| `data.entity.read` | Read entity data | Yes |
| `data.entity.create` | Create new entity records | Yes |
| `data.entity.update` | Update entity records | Yes |
| `data.entity.delete` | Delete entity records | Yes |

Filterable permissions support the `filter` effect for row-level security, but in the patterns above we used simple `allow` without row-level restrictions.

## Schema Permissions

Schema operations are protected by system permissions:

```typescript
{
  permission: "system.schema.read",
  effect: "allow",
  policies: [],
}
```

Protects:
- `GET /api/system/schema` - Get current schema
- `GET /api/data/schema` - Get data schema

## Testing Permissions

Create test users to verify access control:

```typescript
import { createApp } from "bknd";

const app = createApp({
  connection: { url: "file:test.db" },
  config: {
    data: schema.toJSON(),
    auth: {
      enabled: true,
      jwt: {
        secret: "test-secret",
      },
      roles: [
        // Your roles configuration
      ],
    },
  },
});
await app.build();

// Create test data
await app.em.mutator("posts").insertMany([
  { title: "Public Post", published: true },
  { title: "Private Post", published: false },
]);

// Test as guest
const guestResponse = await app.server.request("/api/data/entity/posts");
const guestPosts = await guestResponse.json();
console.log("Guest sees:", guestPosts.data); // All posts, including unpublished

// Create authenticated user
const user = await app.createUser({
  email: "user@example.com",
  password: "password123",
});

const token = await app.auth.login(user.email, "password123");

// Test authenticated user can update any post
await app.server.request("/api/data/entity/posts/2", {
  method: "PATCH",
  headers: {
    Authorization: `Bearer ${token}`,
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ title: "Updated" }),
});
// Succeeds even though user didn't create the post
```

## DOs and DON'Ts

**DO:**
- Use `implicit_allow: false` for production roles to require explicit permissions
- Define separate roles for guests and authenticated users
- Test with both guest and authenticated contexts
- Use `is_default: true` for the guest role
- Leverage policy conditions for specific entity targeting

**DON'T:**
- Use `implicit_allow: true` unless you truly need all access
- Forget to set `is_default: true` for the guest role
- Mix allow and deny in the same permission (deny takes precedence)
- Skip testing edge cases (what happens with null user context?)
- Assume that role-based permissions alone handle row-level filtering

## Common Issues

**Guests can't access anything:**
- Ensure `auth.enabled: true` (required for Guard)
- Check `is_default: true` is set on a role
- Verify `implicit_allow: false` (explicit permissions required)

**Authenticated users can't create posts:**
- Check that the user role has the `data.entity.create` permission with an allow effect
- Verify the policy condition matches the entity name

**Public endpoints returning 403:**
- Verify guest role has the required permission
- Check that no deny policies are conflicting

## Next Steps

- **[Auth](auth)** - Configure authentication strategies
- **[Data Schema](data-schema)** - Define your data model
- **[Query](query)** - Learn the query system

